peech review consolidates quotation marks, capitals, commas/reporting clauses and end marks."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising direct speech?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named comma and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the comma and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact."],"question":"A learner is practising direct speech. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries direct speech.","options":["Say the writing is good without naming any words.","Missing commas or end marks inside/outside quotes are common errors to hunt.","Point to exact words in the sentence that show direct speech and say what they do.","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event."],"question":"Read: \"After the blackout, Tess lit a candle and read the crumpled note.\" Which choice best uses direct speech from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Missing commas or end marks inside/outside quotes are common errors to hunt.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","The bird warned, \"Run to the ridge!\""],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about direct speech?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding.","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue.","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise comma and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea.","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake.","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess.","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Missing commas or end marks inside/outside quotes are common errors to hunt.","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about direct speech.","Retype the worked model as the final answer.","Change fonts and claim the work is new."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of direct speech to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating direct speech on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: The bird warned, \"Run to the ridge!\" Reasoning: I used direct speech by attending to comma and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with direct speech, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Missing commas or end marks inside/outside quotes are common errors to hunt. Correction: The bird warned, \"Run to the ridge!\" Why: the correction restores accurate use of direct speech.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y3-w33-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise direct speech.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use direct speech with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify comma and explain why it matters.","I can practise capital independently on a new prompt.","I can notice end marks needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Direct speech review consolidates quotation marks, capitals, commas/reporting clauses and end marks. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Direct speech review consolidates quotation marks, capitals, commas/reporting clauses and end marks.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about direct speech?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCorrect: Lea said, \"The ford is higher up.\" The comma sits before the quote; the quoted sentence starts with a capital.\n\nTRY FIRST\nPunctuate: the bird warned run to the ridge\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe bird warned, \"Run to the ridge!\"","explain":"Direct speech review consolidates quotation marks, capitals, commas/reporting clauses and end marks.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Missing commas or end marks inside/outside quotes are common errors to hunt. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Direct speech punctuation with comma and capital.","caption":"Quoted sentences need end marks too.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Speech marks</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">&quot;The ford is higher up.&quot;</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Reporting comma</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Lea said,</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Correct: Lea said, \"The ford is higher up.\" The comma sits before the quote; the quoted sentence starts with a capital. Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Correct: Lea said, \"The ford is higher up.\" The comma sits before the quote; the quoted sentence starts with a capital. Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","A claim unrelated to the text."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about direct speech?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCorrect: Lea said, \"The ford is higher up.\" The comma sits before the quote; the quoted sentence starts with a capital. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Excited quote with complete punctuation.","caption":"Hunt missing commas and capitals.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Urgent quote</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">&quot;Run to the ridge!&quot;</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Common miss</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">missing comma before quote</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about direct speech?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nMissing commas or end marks inside/outside quotes are common errors to hunt. Here is a more productive model: Correct: Lea said, \"The ford is higher up.\" The comma sits before the quote; the quoted sentence starts with a capital.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe bird warned, \"Run to the ridge!\"","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example a